# CNODE Configurer

## Overview
This project is to configure the cnode hardware and software.
This is a PySide6 application that will help the user configure the cnode hardware and software.

### Architecture
The application will be composed of the following parts:
- Main Window
- Menu Bar - Menu bar with the following items:
  - Connection - Connect to the cnode_handler
  - Settings - Settings for the application
  - Help - Help for the application
  - About - About the application
- Topbar (Top of the page):
  - left most: Logo of the application
  - second left: Connection button - shows the connection status
    - on click, a popup will show the connection status
    - if not connected, a popup will ask for the connection
  - right: 
    - button: switch between maintenance mode and production mode
    - Notification button - shows the number of notifications
      - on click, a popup will show the notifications
    - right most button: Account button - shows the account information
      - on click, a popup will show the account information
      - if not logged in, a popup will ask for the login credentials
  - Middle: Current Mode (Maintenance or Production)
  - Color - Changes as per the mode (default accent for production mode and yellow for maintenance mode)
- Sidebar: will contain the following tabs:
  - Dashboard (more in dashboard.mdc)
  - Configure (this will drop down) (only available in Maintenance mode) (Configure the workflows, detection profiles, cameras, etc.)
    - Workflows - Workflows for the cnode module (more in workflows.mdc)
    - Detection Profiles - Detection profiles for the cnode module (more in detection_profiles.mdc)
    - Cameras - Resizing, mirroring, flipping, etc. (more in cameras.mdc)
  - Updates - Updates for the application (more in updates.mdc)

## Code convetions
- we will connect to the cnode device using hostname : cnode1.local:xxxx where xxxx is the port number of the relevant service 
- Ports and services onboard:
  - 8060: Camera Streamer
  - 8061: Detection Streamer
  - 9001: Supervisor
  - 5672: RabbitMQ
  - 22: SSH
  - 8000: cnode_manager - this is a s django erver to store data related to the cnode device, will make it eventually, so do make likely calls for endpoints of that service.
  - save the connections in a file called connections.json for the connection manager to use.

## Code Structure
- Make proper classes for each module
- keep certain objects accessible for all modules like Connection
- Make managers like ConnectionManager, NotificationManager, AccountManager, etc.
- When needed use threads, timers, queues, etc. eg: camera streamers, status streamers, etc.
- Use proper logging and error handling
- Use proper UI design and styling
    
## Considerations
This is a standalone application that will run on the user's machine connected to another compute via ethernet where cnode_handler will be running.

Most of the configs will be saved to the cnode_manager server, the db api will then put the files in their respective places and store a state of change in the db. This applies to workflows.json, cnode_handler.json, detection templates etc.